#
# Rebuild the registered modules for the kernel the NEXT boot will use, while this one still runs and
# the network it needs is still up. The ordinary Update button reaches no upgrade hook - it puts the
# new kernel in /boot and reboots - but rc.reboot, rc.halt and rc.shutdown all run `rc.syshook stop`
# before anything is torn down, so this is the moment that always comes (os-xgs-npu, 2026-10-02).
# Numbered below 80-freebsd, which takes the network with it.
#
# Bounded: a fetch that will not finish must not hold the machine. And never the reason a reboot does
# not happen. With nothing registered, or nothing changed, it is a few reads.
: ${FOLLOW:=/usr/local/opnsense/scripts/kernelfollow/follow.sh}
: ${LIMIT:=600}
[ -f "${FOLLOW}" ] || exit 0

# THE FALL-BACK FIRST, ON ITS OWN. Where a module the network needs is not built for the kernel in
# /boot and is built for kernel.old, of the same FreeBSD major release, follow.sh's own
# pin_old_kernel() sends the next boot to kernel.old, and where none needs it unpin_old_kernel() lets
# go of the one this plugin set: PINONLY=1, which waits for no lock and fetches nothing, so it is done
# whatever becomes of the run below. Until 2026-11-06 that run was all there was, and it arms the
# fall-back only once it holds follow.sh's lock. Where another run held it - a five-minute run
# started while a major upgrade's kernel was still a staged set, which arms nothing, still fetching
# over a slow line - this one waited for it until the time limit cut it off, and the line below said
# that the next boot took kernel.old with nothing armed. That boot took the new kernel with a module
# built for the old one. A minute is far more than the few reads and the one nextboot this takes.
pin() { PINONLY=1 /usr/bin/timeout -k 5 60 /bin/sh "${FOLLOW}" > /dev/null 2>&1; }
pin

/usr/bin/timeout -k 10 "${LIMIT}" /bin/sh "${FOLLOW}" > /dev/null 2>&1
rc=$?
[ ${rc} -eq 0 ] && exit 0

# What is so now, asked again rather than taken from the answer above, which the wait can overtake:
# the run lets the fall-back go as soon as every module the network needs matches, and can still be
# cut off on another module's build after that; and os-xgs-npu's follower clears nextboot once it has
# rebuilt its own module, whoever armed it (ONE FALL-BACK FOR THE WHOLE HOST, in follow.sh), which its
# five-minute run can do while this one waits - so a fall-back still needed is armed again here, and
# since 2026-11-06 the one this plugin set is let go where none needs it any more. Only the answer
# is said, and kernel.old only where it is armed. Until 2026-11-06 a run cut off here was said to
# leave one the network needs on kernel.old, armed or not. The answer that none needs it is 3 since
# the same day, a status sh never exits with: it was 2, which sh exits with on an error of its own -
# a variable not set, a syntax error - and such an error was read here as every module the network
# needs built for /boot/kernel.
pin
case $? in
0) FOUND="the next boot takes kernel.old, which a module the network needs is built for" ;;
3) FOUND="every module the network needs that this plugin follows is built for /boot/kernel" ;;
*) FOUND="this plugin did not send the next boot to kernel.old" ;;
esac
if [ ${rc} -eq 124 ] || [ ${rc} -eq 137 ]; then
	/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err \
	    "at shutdown: follow.sh was cut off after ${LIMIT}s; ${FOUND}"
else
	/usr/bin/logger -t kernelfollow -p daemon.err \
	    "at shutdown: a module could not be rebuilt (see the entries above); ${FOUND}"
fi
exit 0
